# How much of the world's forest have we lost since the last ice age?
subtitle: "Guess how much of the world's forest is gone since the ice age, then how much of that went in the last century, and where forests are growing back."
tags: ["earth","history","geography"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Ten thousand years of chopping\" note \"How much is gone?\""

## How much of the world's forest have we lost since the last ice age?
text: {"font_size":34}
use: pie_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Lost
      - ' '
      - Still forest
    values:
      - [2, 0, 4]
      - [1, 1, 4]
    stage: 0
    percent: false
    box:
      x: 380
      'y': 180
      w: 610
      h: 490
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: owid_third
      title: The world has lost one-third of its forests, but an end to deforestation is possible
      authors: Hannah Ritchie (Our World in Data)
      year: 2021
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/world-lost-one-third-forests
      finding: '6 billion hectares of forest 10,000 years ago, 4 billion today: one third lost, an area twice the size of the United States; half of the loss happened from 8,000 BC to 1900, the other half since 1900; driven by the expansion of farmland'
    - id: owid_area
      title: Forest area
      authors: Our World in Data, from the UN Food and Agriculture Organization (2025)
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/forest-area-km
      finding: 'forest area 1990 to 2025: Europe (including Russia) 998 to 1,039 million hectares (+41 million); South America 1,028 to 849 million (−180 million)'
    - id: owid_transition
      title: Humans destroyed forests for thousands of years — we can become the first generation that achieves a world in which forests expand
      authors: Max Roser (Our World in Data)
      year: 2021
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/global-forest-transition
      finding: forests have expanded in Europe and North America since 1990, as higher crop yields reduced the need for farmland

    icon tree size 170 set twemoji x 150 y 560 icon_look picture of "deciduous tree" hidden true
    text third "⅓ gone" #b5482e cites ["owid_third"] x 150 y 420 font_size 64 hidden true
    text usa2 "= 2 × USA" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_third"] x 150 y 310 font_size 44 hidden true
    icon farms size 100 set twemoji x 100 y 310 icon_look picture of cow hidden true
    icon farms2 size 100 set twemoji x 200 y 310 icon_look picture of "sheaf of rice" hidden true
    text century "1900s ≈ 9,000 years before" #b5482e cites ["owid_third"] x 560 y 655 font_size 44 hidden true
    icon eu_t1 size 130 set twemoji x 260 y 460 icon_look picture of "evergreen tree" hidden true
    icon eu_t2 size 190 set twemoji x 420 y 490 icon_look picture of "evergreen tree" hidden true
    icon eu_t3 size 260 set twemoji x 620 y 520 icon_look picture of "evergreen tree" hidden true
    text eu_t "Europe +41 million ha" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_area"] x 500 y 300 font_size 54 hidden true
    text eu_de "> Germany" #2f6b8f x 850 y 390 font_size 48 hidden true
    text sa "−180 M ha" #b5482e cites ["owid_area"] x 840 y 490 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s1900 "1900+" #b5482e x 920 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon stump size 90 set twemoji x 850 y 625 or ["log"] icon_look picture of wood hidden true
    text incl "(incl. Russia), 1990–2025" #5e584e cites ["owid_area"] x 500 y 220 font_size 34 hidden true
    box o1 "A tenth" x 180 y 560 width 250 height 86 font_size 44 hidden true
    box o2 "A quarter" x 180 y 450 width 250 height 86 font_size 44 hidden true
    box o3 "Half" x 180 y 340 width 250 height 86 font_size 44 hidden true
    group opts members ["o1","o2","o3"] hidden true
    text sa1 "S. America" #b5482e x 840 y 555 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "How much forest have we lost?"

When the last ice age ended, ten thousand years ago, forests covered more than half of the world's habitable land. How much of that forest have we lost since?
    draw tree

    ask "Drag the slice's edge to your guess." on slice_1 store g default "60"
        right: Yes: we have lost about a third.
        wrong: It's about a third of those ancient forests.

Two billion hectares gone: an area twice the size of the United States.
    draw third usa2

    erase usa2

Almost all of it was cleared for farms.
    draw farms farms2

    erase third usa2 farms farms2 tree

So, a second question. Of all that loss, how much came after 1900?
    draw opts

    ask "Tap your answer." choose ["o1","o2","o3"] answer o3 store h
        right: Half. In about a century we cleared as much forest as in the nine thousand years before.
        wrong: Think of the twentieth century's machines and billions more mouths.

    erase o1 o2

    animate stage 1 duration 2.5

    draw s1900

Why so fast? Billions more people to feed, and machines that clear land in days.
    draw century

    erase slice_1 slice_2 slice_3 century s1900 o3

But here is the twist. In Europe, counting Russia, forests are growing again: forty-one million hectares since 1990.
    draw eu_t1 eu_t2 eu_t3 eu_t incl

That is more than the whole of Germany, turned back into forest.
    draw eu_de

Why? Farms grow more food on less land, so old fields go back to trees. South America, meanwhile, is still losing them fast.
    draw stump sa1 sa

So we cut down a third of the world's forests, half of it in one century, and some places have already turned the corner.
    highlight eu_t

